  "question": {
    "text": "It is given that the events $A$ and $B$ are such that $P(A)=\\frac{1}{4}, P\\left(\\frac{A}{B}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $P\\left(\\frac{B}{A}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}$. Then $P(B)$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "rIZvl",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{1}{6}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "LIgta",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{1}{3}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "JoW2v",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{2}{3}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "XrXkb",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{1}{2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\frac{P(A \\cap B)}{P(B)}=\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{P(A \\cap B)}{P(A)}=\\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n& \\text { Hence } \\frac{P(A)}{P(B)}=\\frac{3}{4} . \\quad(\\text { But } P(A)=1 / 4) \\\\\n& \\Rightarrow P(B)=\\frac{1}{3} .\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
